{{History of Hungary}}
[[Hungary]] in its modern (post-1946) borders roughly corresponds to the [[Great Hungarian Plain]] (the [[Carpathian basin|Carpathian Basin]]) in [[Central Europe]].

During the [[Iron Age Europe|Iron Age]], it was located at the crossroads between the cultural spheres of [[Scythians|Scythian]] tribes (such as [[Agathyrsi]], [[Cimmerians]]), the [[Celts|Celtic tribes]] (such as the [[Scordisci]], [[Boii (Pannonia)|Boii]] and [[Veneti (Gaul)|Veneti]]), [[Illyrians|Dalmatian tribes]] (such as the [[Dalmatae]], [[Histri]] and [[Liburnians|Liburni]]) and the [[Germanic peoples|Germanic tribes]] (such as the [[Lugii]], [[Marcomanni]]). In 44 BC, the [[Sarmatians]], [[Iazyges]] moved into the [[Great Hungarian Plain]]. In 8 AD, the western part of the territory (the so-called [[Transdanubia]]) of modern Hungary formed part of [[Pannonia]], a province of the [[Roman Empire]]. Roman control collapsed with the Hunnic invasions of 370–410, the [[Huns]] created a significant empire based in present-day Hungary. In 453 they reached the height of their expansion under [[Attila the Hun]]. After the death of Attila, the empire collapsed in 455, and Pannonia became part of the [[Ostrogothic Kingdom]]. The western part of the Carpathian Basin was occupied by the [[Lombards|Longobards]] and the eastern part by the [[Gepids]]. In 567, the [[Pannonian Avars|Avars]] occupied the territory ruled by the Gepids. In 568, the Longobards moved to Italy from Transdanubia, and the Avars also occupied that territory, Khagan [[Bayan I]] established the Avar Khaganate. The Avars were defeated by the [[Carolingian Empire|Franks]] and [[Bulgars]], and their steppe-empire ended around 822.

The [[Hungarians]] took possession of the Carpathian Basin between 862 and 895, and the [[Principality of Hungary]] was established in the late 9th century by [[Álmos]] and his son [[Árpád]] through the [[Hungarian conquest of the Carpathian Basin|conquest of the Carpathian Basin]], the Hungarians secured the territory by the [[Battle of Pressburg]] in 907. The Christian [[Kingdom of Hungary]] was established in 1000 under King [[Stephen I of Hungary|Saint Stephen]], ruled by the [[Árpád dynasty]] for the following three centuries. In the [[Kingdom of Hungary (1000–1301)|high medieval period]], the kingdom expanded to the [[Adriatic Sea|Adriatic]] coast and entered a [[Croatia in personal union with Hungary|personal union]] with [[Kingdom of Croatia (925–1102)|Croatia]] in 1102. In 1241, Hungary was [[First Mongol invasion of Hungary|invaded]] by the [[Mongols]] under [[Batu Khan]]. The medieval Kingdom of Hungary was a European power, reaching [[Kingdom of Hungary (1301–1526)|its height in the 14th-15th century]]. Hungary bore the brunt of the [[Ottoman wars in Europe]] during the 15th century. After a long period of [[Ottoman–Hungarian wars|Ottoman wars]], Hungary's forces were defeated at the [[Battle of Mohács]] and its capital was [[Siege of Buda (1541)|captured]] in 1541, opening roughly a 150 years long period when the country was divided into three parts: [[Royal Hungary]] loyal to the [[House of Habsburg|Habsburgs]], [[Ottoman Hungary]] and the semi-independent [[Principality of Transylvania (1570–1711)|Principality of Transylvania]]. The reunited Hungary came under Habsburg rule at the turn of the 18th century, fighting a [[Rákóczi's War of Independence|war of independence]] in 1703–1711, and a [[Hungarian Revolution of 1848|war of independence]] in 1848–1849 until a [[Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867|compromise]] allowed the formation of the [[Austria-Hungary|Austro-Hungarian Monarchy]] in 1867, a [[Great power|major power]] into the early 20th century. The [[Croatian–Hungarian Settlement]] of 1868 settled the political status of the [[Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia]] within the [[Lands of the Crown of Saint Stephen]] which was the official name for the Hungarian territories of the Dual Monarchy.

Austria-Hungary collapsed after [[World War I]], and the subsequent [[Treaty of Trianon]] in 1920 established Hungary's current borders, resulting in the loss of 72% of its historical territory, 58% of its population, and [[Hungarian diaspora|32% of its ethnic Hungarians]]. Two-thirds of territory of the Kingdom of Hungary was ceded to [[History of Czechoslovakia (1918–1938)|Czechoslovakia]], the [[Kingdom of Romania]], the [[Kingdom of Yugoslavia|Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes]], the [[First Austrian Republic]], the [[Second Polish Republic]] and the [[Kingdom of Italy]]. A short-lived [[First Hungarian Republic|People's Republic]] was declared. It was followed by a restored [[Kingdom of Hungary (1920–1946)|Kingdom of Hungary]] but was governed by a [[Regent of Hungary|regent]], [[Miklós Horthy]]. He officially represented the [[Holy Crown of Hungary|Hungarian monarchy]] of [[Charles I of Austria|Charles IV, Apostolic King of Hungary]]. Between 1938 and 1941, Hungary recovered part of its lost territories and joined the Axis Powers. During [[Hungary in World War II|World War II]] Hungary came under [[Operation Panzerfaust|German occupation]] in 1944, then under [[Soviet Union|Soviet]] occupation until the end of the war. After World War II, the [[Second Hungarian Republic]] was established within Hungary's current-day borders as a [[Socialist state|socialist]] [[Hungarian People's Republic|People's Republic]], lasting from 1949 to the [[end of communism in Hungary]] in [[End of communism in Hungary (1989)|1989]]. The [[Third Republic of Hungary]] was established under an amended version of the [[Hungarian Constitution of 1949|constitution of 1949]], with a [[Constitution of Hungary|new constitution]] adopted in 2011. Hungary joined the [[European Union]] [[2004 enlargement of the European Union|in 2004]].

