[[File:NCI Visuals Food Hamburger.jpg|300px|thumb|Hamburger profile showing the typical ingredients: bread, vegetables, and ground meat.]]
[[File:Burger and fries (1).jpg|300px|thumb|Open hamburger with [[processed cheese|cheese]] and [[French fries|fries]] served in an American [[diner]]]]

Originally just a [[ground beef]] [[patty]], as it is still interpreted in multiple languages,{{efn|Eg. {{Langx|it|[[:it:hamburger|hamburger]]}}.}} and the name "hamburger" may be a reference to ground beef sold in [[Hamburg]];<ref>{{cite web|last=Barksdale|first=Nate|date=29 August 2014|title=Where Hamburgers Began—and How They Became an Iconic American Food|website=History.com|url=https://www.history.com/news/hamburger-helpers-the-history-of-americas-favorite-sandwich}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Avey|first=Tory|date=24 April 2024|title=Do You Actually Know Where Hamburgers Originated?|website=Parade.com|url=https://parade.com/61481/toriavey/where-did-hamburgers-originate/}}</ref> [[Hamburger#Claims of invention|evidence also suggests]] that the [[United States]] was the first country to create the "hamburger" as it is known today, where two slices of bread and a [[ground beef]] patty were combined into a "[[hamburger sandwich]]" and sold as such. The hamburger soon included all of its current characteristic trimmings, including onions, lettuce, and sliced [[pickled cucumber|pickles]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://heritagefoods.com/blogs/news/the-history-of-the-hamburger | title=The History of the Hamburger | date=July 2, 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://historycooperative.org/who-invented-the-hamburger/ | title=Who Invented the Hamburger? A Surprising History Behind the Origin of Hamburger &#124; History Cooperative | date=October 16, 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.foxnews.com/lifestyle/meet-american-made-us-flip-hamburgers-louis-lassen-danish-immigrant-street-wagon-cook.amp | title=Meet the American who made us flip for hamburgers, Louis Lassen, Danish immigrant street-wagon cook &#124; Fox News | website=[[Fox News]] }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://vocal.media/lifehack/burger-inventor-s-story | title=Burger Inventor's Story }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.cracked.com/article_42749_5-people-and-places-that-claim-to-have-invented-the-hamburger.html | title=5 People and Places That Claim to Have Invented the Hamburger | date=July 4, 2024 }}</ref>

There is still some controversy over the origin of the hamburger – mainly because its two basic ingredients, bread and beef, have been prepared and consumed separately for many years in many countries prior to their combination. However, after various controversies in the 20th century, including a nutritional controversy in the late 1990s, the burger is now readily identified with the United States, as well as a particular style of American cuisine, namely [[fast food]].<ref name="ilustra">{{Cite book|url= https://books.google.com/books?id=K-DfAAAAMAAJ|title=Hamburger Heaven: The Illustrated History of the Hamburger|last=Tennyson|first=Jeffrey|publisher=Hyperion|year=1993|isbn=978-1-56282982-7}}</ref> Along with [[fried chicken]] and [[apple pie]], the hamburger has become a culinary icon in the United States.<ref name="Edge">{{Cite book | last = Edge | first = John T. |title= Hamburgers and Fries: An American Story |edition= 1st |year= 2005 |publisher= Putnam Adult |location= New York |isbn= 0-399-15274-1 |url= https://archive.org/details/hamburgersfriesa00edge }}</ref><ref name= "amer">{{Cite book | first = C. | last = Counihan |title= Food in the USA |edition= 1st |date=July 2002 |publisher= Routledge |location= New York |isbn= 0-415-93232-7}}</ref>

The hamburger's international popularity is the result of the larger [[globalization]] of food<ref name="Global" /> that also includes the rise in global popularity of other national dishes, including the Italian [[pizza]], Chinese [[fried rice]], and Japanese [[sushi]]. The hamburger has spread from continent to continent, perhaps because it matches familiar elements in different culinary cultures.<ref name="McGlobal" /> This global culinary culture has been produced, in part, by the concept of selling processed food, first launched in the 1920s by the [[White Castle (restaurant)|White Castle]] restaurant chain and its founder Edgar Waldo "Billy" Ingram and then refined by [[McDonald's]] in the 1940s.<ref name= "Smith" /><ref name="Arcs" /> This global expansion provides economic points of comparison like the [[Big Mac Index]],<ref name= "BigIndex" /> by which one can compare the purchasing power of different countries where the [[Big Mac]] hamburger is sold.

